markdown <<MARKDOWN
## Parsing JSON

#### To use data from a file or API in your app, you first need to turn it into something JavaScript can work with.

#### Parsing means carefully looking at the data, figuring out what type of data each piece is (text, number, list, etc.), and deciding what information is important for the user to see.

---

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Parsing JSON</strong> means analyzing a string of JSON symbols to determine its structure and break it down into smaller, meaningful components.<br><br>
When we parse JSON, we turn the raw data into JavaScript objects usable by our program.
</p>
</div>

---

## Why parse first?

#### It’s important to parse the JSON data before designing and coding the app because you need to understand what information is available (and how it’s organized) before deciding what to display.

---
## Example: One Card, One JSON Object

#### Here is an example of a single JSON object. A JSON object stores information in **key–value pairs**, where each piece of data has a label (called a key) and a value.

#### Notice it contains multiple keys with different types of values, including text (strings), numbers, and an array.

<div style="display: flex; gap: 30px; align-items: flex-start; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 30px 0;">

  <!-- Card Image -->
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 300px; text-align: center;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/3b0a548c969ad731df7749c3157eb7b8-image-1772469838212.png"
         alt="Music card example"
         style="width: 75%; max-width: 350px; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 6px 14px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);">
    <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 12pt;"><strong>Card Example</strong></p>
  </div>

  <!-- Faux JSON Box -->
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 350px;">
    <div style="background-color: #f4f4f4; border-radius: 10px; border: 2pt solid black; padding: 18px; box-sizing: border-box;">
      <p style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0; white-space: pre;">
{
  "track": "Girls Like Us",  
  "artist": "Zoe Wees",
  "image_url": "zoe_wees.png",
  "popularity": 12,
  "genres": ["Pop rock", "Dance"]
}

</p>
</div>
</div>

</div>

<p style = "font-size:14pt;">Even though this object includes several keys, an app might only display a few of them on a card (like <strong>track</strong>, <strong>artist</strong>, and <strong>image_url</strong>) and leave others (like <strong>popularity</strong> or <strong>genres</strong>) for sorting, filtering, or a details page.</p>

---

<h3>✅ Check Your Understanding</h3>

<p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.4;">
A single JSON object can contain lots of keys, but an app usually chooses only some of them to display.
This example app shows the <strong>image</strong>, <strong>track</strong>, and <strong>artist</strong>.
</p>

<p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.4;">
<strong>Question:</strong> Which keys from the JSON object are <em>not</em> shown in the app, and why might the app choose to leave them out?
</p>

<details style="background-color: #f2f2f2; border-radius: 10px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 15px 0;">
  <summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14pt;">
    🔍 Click to reveal the answer
  </summary>

  <p style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.4;">
    The keys <strong>popularity</strong> and <strong>genres</strong> are not shown in the app. The app might leave them out to keep the card simple and focused, or save those details for a different feature—like sorting/filtering, or a “more info” screen.
  </p>
</details>

---
